## .dockerignore — tells Docker what to exclude when building the image
## Works exactly like .gitignore but for docker build
## Without this, Docker copies everything into the image including the database,
## git history, logs, and notebooks — bloating the image unnecessarily

## Database — never bake this into the image
## It's mounted as a volume at runtime so it stays on the host
## Including it would add ~50MB to every image build unnecessarily
*.duckdb

## Git history — the image doesn't need version control history
## Excluding this shaves significant size off the image
.git
.gitignore

## Python cache — compiled bytecode auto-regenerates when the app runs
## No point shipping it in the image
__pycache__
*.pyc
*.pyo

## Environment files — these often contain secrets like API keys
## Never let these get into a Docker image
.env
.env.*

## Local development files — not needed on the server
logs/
data/
notebooks/
tests/
docs/
api/
monitoring/
scripts/

## Mac junk files
.DS_Store

## The Docker files themselves don't need to be inside the image
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
